<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Giyanti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1755">1755</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-javanese-war-of-succession">Third Javanese War of Succession</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Giyanti, signed on 13 February 1755, divided the Mataram Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> into two realms, Surakarta and Yogyakarta. Brokered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, it ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-javanese-war-of-succession">Third Javanese War of Succession</a> by recognising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-iii">Pakubuwana III</a> at Surakarta and the rebel prince Mangkubumi as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-i">Hamengkubuwana I</a> of Yogyakarta. Both courts still exist.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-giyanti.jpg" alt="Treaty of Giyanti" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original document is currently saved in National Library of Indonesia. … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MsGiyanti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
